{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRModulusFold","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Basic"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.phi_node_is_qi_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.phi_node_is_qi_two","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.lean#L149-L154","doc":"The `φ`-node quantum dimension is the companion's Chebyshev fold value\n`qi φ 2 = φ`: the `((1+√5)/2)` in `colored_modulus_phi_node` is exactly the\nmark-2 quantum integer at the first fold. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.colored_modulus_phi_node","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.colored_modulus_phi_node","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.lean#L138-L147","doc":"**Colored modulus identity at a `[α] = φ` node** (marks 2, 3; first flat\nconnection): `A₁(α)²·D²(ζ¹) = 4·φ²`, `φ = (1+√5)/2 = [2]_{ζ¹} = qi φ 2`.  Together\nwith `amplitude_first` (the `[α] = 1` nodes, `A₁²·D² = 4`) and `qi φ 5 = 0`, this\ncloses the colored amplitude at every Ê₈ node: `A₁(α)²·D²(ζ¹) = 4·[α]²_{ζ¹}`,\ni.e. `W_λ ∝ [mark λ]²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.sin_sq_four_pi_div_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.sin_sq_four_pi_div_five","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.lean#L131-L136","doc":"`sin²(4π/5) = (10 − 2√5)/16` — by reflection `sin(4π/5) = sin(π/5)`\n(the mark-4 node, `[4] = 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.sin_sq_three_pi_div_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.sin_sq_three_pi_div_five","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.lean#L124-L129","doc":"`sin²(3π/5) = (10 + 2√5)/16` — by reflection `sin(3π/5) = sin(2π/5)`\n(the mark-3 node, `[3] = φ`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.amplitude_second","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.amplitude_second","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.lean#L100-L111","doc":"**Bare amplitude, second flat connection**: `((2/√5)·sin(2π/5))² = 4/(10 − 2√5)`\n— i.e. `A₂(1)² = 4/D²(ζ²)`, the Galois-conjugate vacuum modular-S element.  Reduces\nto the companion's proved `vacuum_modulus_identity_second`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.amplitude_first","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.amplitude_first","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.lean#L90-L98","doc":"**Bare amplitude, first flat connection**: `((2/√5)·sin(π/5))² = 4/(10 + 2√5)`\n— i.e. `A₁(1)² = 4/D²(ζ¹)`, the vacuum modular-S element `S_{00}(ζ¹)`.  Reduces to\nthe companion's proved `vacuum_modulus_identity_first`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.sin_sq_two_pi_div_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.sin_sq_two_pi_div_five","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.lean#L76-L88","doc":"**Second flat connection** (Galois conjugate): `sin²(2π/5) = (10 + 2√5)/16`.\nVia the sine double angle `sin(2π/5) = 2·sin(π/5)·cos(π/5)` and\n`cos(π/5) = (1+√5)/4`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.sin_sq_pi_div_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.sin_sq_pi_div_five","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneLRAmplitudeReal.lean#L65-L74","doc":"**First flat connection**: `sin²(π/5) = (10 − 2√5)/16`.  Via\n`cos(π/5) = (1+√5)/4` (Mathlib) and the Pythagorean identity. ","signatureChunk":0}]}